            <ArticleTitle>[Atraumatic hip pain in young adults].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>54-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.semerg.2014.07.012</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1138-3593(14)00326-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Hip pain in the young adult is a disabling pathophysiological process may be related to multiple etiologies. The process must be determined in order to make a diagnosis and follow-up treatment. The case is presented of a 29 year old woman with anemia, atraumatic hip pain on the right side, and a limp of one month onset. The differential diagnosis includes infectious, rheumatological, tumor, avascular necrosis of hip, hip impingement, hip dysplasia, osteoarthritis and other syndromes.</AbstractText>
